REST style architecture :- (architectural principle of the web)
   1. States and functionalities are being devided into distributed resources.
   2.Every resource is  uniquely addressable and with uniform and minimium commands ( example- protocol-http, request type- GET/PUT/POST etc..)
   3.The protocol is client/ server, stateless and support caching.
   
   
   In HTTP request , request header contains 
   'Content-Type'='appplication/json' => data sent from client to server in JSON format.
   'Accept'='appication/XML' (or dataTyle in $.ajax)=> data sent from server to client in XML format. 
   
REST constrains:-
1.Client server -> In application , there should be client to send request and a server to response to the request.
2.Stateless-> Server should not store any information related to client. Hence each request can be treated as a new request 
              and hence stateless.
3. Cacheable:-  We can cache data that does not change frequently, so that client does not need to generate request each time.
4. Uniform Interface:- Any data that is requested by client is called resource. So for getting any resource, Web API exposes uniform 
                       URI (uniform resoure interface) .
                       
Http Verbs:
1.Get:- get request
2.Post- create new resource
3. Put- Update existing resource.
4. Delete- Delete a resource.

When to use WCF over Web API:-
    WCF needs lot more configuration to make it REST full, where as Web API is restfull by default.
    When any application needs to use protocol except http, then we should go with WCF. (in WCF we need to define two end points for same serviece.)
    
ApiController resides under System.Web.http namespace.   

Content Negotiation:-
   Whenever a client send a request, it has to set 'Accept' property of request header. Ex- Accept: application/json (default is json)
Based on this property server will send data either in xml or json or any other format as specified in the Accept header property.

**Custom methods names:--
     By default Web API calls requests that matches the verb type. Ex- if it is a get request , web API will look for Get or GetAntyName.
     If anything found, it will server the request even though methos signature is different.
     
  ** Quesry string parameter:-
     localhost:8080/api/employee?gender=male       is the URL to filter data by gender, by reading value of gender from query string.
     
** FromBody & FromUri
    if input paramter of API method is of simple data type (like int, float,bool etc), web API tries to read those values either fron
    route data or from query string, but if input parameter is a complex type (ex- Employee, Customer etc) then web API tries to 
    read it from request body.
    
    FromUri attribute forces Web API to read data either from query string or from route data.
    
** calling Web API from jQuery
    $.ajax('http://localhost:8080/api/employee'). This will work if both client & servie are into same project.

** Same origin policy:-    Browser allows webpage to make AJAX call inside same domain (i.e in same app domain, main URL must match including protocol type, port number, site name etc) and browser security 
                        blocks a AJAX call if AJAX is being called to any other domain. This policy is called Same Origin Policy.

** cross domain AJAX:-
     We can fix cross AJAX problem by below two ways.
     1. Using JSONP (JSON with Padding)
     2.Enabling CORS (Cross Origin Resource Sharing).

JSONP
---------------
    It wraps output data inside a function.
ex:-
  CallbackFunction({
   "firstName":"Mark",
   "lastName":"xyz",   
  })

1.install WebApiContrib.Formatting.Jsonp from Nuget.
2. add below line of code in App-Start method
        GlobalConfiguration.Configuration.Formatters.Insert(0,  new JsonpMediaTypeFormatter(new JsonMediaTypeFormatter()));
  or
  add below code in RegisterFormatters method in WebApiConfig file
         var jsonpFormatter =   new JsonpMediaTypeFormatter(formatters.JsonFormatter);  
         formatters.Insert(0, jsonpFormatter);  
3.change response format to JSONP in ajax request.
         $.ajax({
            type:"POST",
            url:'...',
            data: JSON.stringify({x:anyObject}),
            contentType:"application/json; charset=utf-8",          
            dataType:"jsonp',                                       
            complete:function(data){},
            error:function(response,error){}
          });


Cross Origin Resource Sharing
---------------------------------------------
1. install package - > Microsoft.AspNet.WebApi.Cors
2. aad below codes in WebApiConfig Register method
        EnableCorsAttribute cors=new EnableCorsAttribute("*","*","*");
        config.EnableCors(cors);
        

